  "assembly": "# Advent of Code 2025, day 9 part 1 (Movie Theater)\n#\n# THE PUZZLE\n# The input is a list of red tiles, each a position X,Y on a floor of square tiles. The answer is the\n# area of the largest rectangle that has red tiles at two opposite corners: for tiles at (x1,y1) and\n# (x2,y2) that is (|x1 - x2| + 1) * (|y1 - y2| + 1) tiles.\n#\n# THE IDEA\n# 496 tiles make 122,760 pairs, and an area is a multiplication the CPU has to do by doubling and adding.\n# So the program only multiplies for pairs that can beat the best area so far.\n#   - A rectangle W wide and H high has area at most ((W + H) / 2) squared. With R = the whole part of\n#     2 * (square root of BEST), a pair with W + H <= R cannot beat BEST.\n#   - W + H - 2 = |x1 - x2| + |y1 - y2|, and that is the larger of |S1 - S2| and |D1 - D2|, where\n#     S = X + Y and D = X - Y. So a pair worth a look is far apart in S or far apart in D.\n#   - The tiles are sorted twice, by S and by D. In a sorted list the pairs farthest apart are those\n#     made of its two ends, so the program walks inwards from both ends and stops as soon as the keys\n#     are no more than R - 2 apart. That is done once in the list sorted by S and once in the other.\n#   - Before that the two ends of each list are tried as a pair: that already gives a good BEST and R.\n# The example: the tiles with the smallest and the largest S are 2,3 (S = 5) and 11,7 (S = 18):\n# area 10 * 5 = 50, so R = 14 (14 * 14 = 196 <= 200). After that only pairs with keys more than 12\n# apart are looked at; none beats 50.\n#\n# THE WHOLE PROGRAM IN PSEUDO CODE\n#     for every tile P: S[P] = X + Y; D[P] = X - Y + 2^31; put P's address in the lists BY_S and BY_D\n#     sort BY_S by S; sort BY_D by D\n#     BEST = 0; R = 0\n#     EVALUATE(first of BY_S, last of BY_S); EVALUATE(first of BY_D, last of BY_D)\n#     SCAN(BY_S); SCAN(BY_D)\n#     write BEST as 15 decimal digits\n#     stop\n#\n#     SCAN(a sorted list):\n#         for LO = the first place; LO before the last place; LO = LO + 1:\n#             for HI = the last place; HI != LO; HI = HI - 1:\n#                 if key(HI) - key(LO) + 2 <= R: break          # and so are all pairs further in\n#                 EVALUATE(tile at LO, tile at HI)\n#             if nothing was evaluated for this LO: return      # the same goes for every later LO\n#\n#     EVALUATE(P, Q):\n#         W = |x(P) - x(Q)| + 1; H = |y(P) - y(Q)| + 1\n#         if W + H <= R: return\n#         AREA = W * H\n#         if AREA > BEST: BEST = AREA; R = the largest number with R * R <= 4 * BEST\n#\n# R is found bit by bit from the top bit down: a bit stays when (R with that bit) squared is not above\n# 4 * BEST. That is 21 multiplications, but BEST changes only a few times.\n#\n# NUMBERS OF 32 BITS AND OF FOUR WORDS\n# A coordinate, S, D, W, H and R are two 16-bit words each, lower word first, and fit the 32-bit\n# registers whole: get32A v loads both words of v into A, and one sum or sub works on all 32 bits.\n# Loading the two words of one register needs the other register for the address. A variable's upper\n# word has the name of the variable with _H. D gets 2^31 added so that it is never below 0.\n# An area is wider still. BEST, AREA, FOUR and the numbers of the decimal digits are four 16-bit words\n# each, 0 the lowest, added word by word with a carry, and subtracted as X + (every bit of Y flipped)\n# + 1; the carry out of the top word then tells whether X >= Y.\n#\n# THE RESULT, when the CPU stops on the Breakpoint at END\n#   0x0100-0x0103  BEST as four 16-bit words, lowest first\n#   0x0110-0x011E  BEST as 15 decimal digits, one per word, most significant first\n#\n# THE INPUT (rule 1 of RULES.md), at 0x2000\n# Four words per tile, in the order of the input: X, then Y, each as two 16-bit words, lower word first\n# (97615 = 0x0001_7D4F is stored as 0x7D4F; 0x0001). After the last tile come two words 0xFFFF.\n# Built-in limits: 2 to 2,000 tiles; coordinates below 1,048,576; the answer below 10^15.\n#\n# THE IDIOMS (v is a variable's address, below 0x1000)\n#     getA v / getB v          A or B = the word at v, upper half 0\n#     putA v                   the word at v = A   (this uses up B)\n#     get32A v / get32B v      A or B = the 32-bit number at v and v + 1; loading one register uses up the other\n#     get32AB v w              A = the 32-bit number at v, B = the one at w (A waits on the stack while B is loaded)\n#     put32A v                 the 32-bit number at v and v + 1 = A   (this uses up B)\n#     getAat p / putAat p      the same as getA / putA for the word the pointer p points at\n#     getAoff p k              A = the word at the address (p + k); p is a variable holding an address\n#     copy v w                 w = v\n#     keepcarry v              v = the upper half of A: the carry of the sum just made\n#     if_eq L / if_neq L       compares A with B as they are now\n#     sub; if_carry L          after A - B: taken when A >= B\n# |a - b| is: sub; when that went below 0, flip every bit (neg) and add 1.\n# What each instruction does exactly is in findings/cpu-instructions.md.\n#\n# MEMORY MAP\n#   0x0100-0x017F  variables            0x2000-  the tiles (496 and the end mark end at 0x27C1)\n#   0x1000-        the program          0x4000-  S and D: for the tile at address P, S is the two words at\n#   0x1800-        the subroutines               P + 0x2000 and D the two words at P + 0x2002\n#   0x1E00-        POWERS: 10^14 down to 1, four words each, then 0\n#   0x1E40-        BITS: 2^20 down to 1, two words each, then 0, 0;  GAPS at 0x1E80: the gaps of the sort, then 0\n#   0x6000-0x67FF  BY_S: the addresses of the tiles, sorted by S    0x6800-0x6FFF  BY_D: the same, sorted by D\n#   0xFF00         where the CPU starts\n\n# ---- The CPU's instructions as symbols (day 1's table without its _32 names, which the CPU ignores) ----\nsymbol nop 0b0000_0000_0000_0000            # the word 0: stops the CPU (the same word as non)\nsymbol ld  0b0010_0000_0000_0000            # load a register from memory (used through the load macro)\nsymbol lia 0b0011_0000_0000_0000            # lia n: A = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol lib 0b0100_0000_0000_0000            # lib n: B = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol sto 0b0101_0000_0000_0000            # store a register to memory (used through the store macro)\n\nmacro  load  { ld  | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # load X Y: X = the word at the address in Y\nmacro  store { sto | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # store X Y: the word at the address in Y = X\n\nsymbol sum    0b0110_0100_0000_0000         # A = A + B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol sub    0b0110_0010_0000_0000         # A = A - B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol shf    0b0110_0001_0000_0000         # shf | k: A = A shifted left by k (0 to 15)\nsymbol and    0b0110_0000_1000_0000         # A = A and B\nsymbol or     0b0110_0000_0100_0000         # A = A or B\nsymbol xor    0b0110_0000_0010_0000         # A = A xor B\nsymbol neg    0b0110_0000_0001_0000         # A = every bit of A flipped\n\nsymbol psh 0b1010_0000_0000_0000            # psh X: push the 16 bits of X\nsymbol pop 0b1011_0000_0000_0000            # pop X: pop 16 bits into X\n\nsymbol jmp  0b1100_0000_0000_0000           # jmp; L: go to L.  jmp c; L: go to L when c holds\nsymbol call 0b1110_0000_0000_0000           # call; L: run the subroutine at L\nsymbol ret  0b1111_0000_0000_0000           # return from a subroutine\n\n# The registers, as load, store, psh and pop name them\nsymbol rgAH 0b1000_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgA  0b0100_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgBH 0b0010_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgB  0b0001_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgSP 0b0000_1000_0000                # the program counter (leave alone)\nsymbol rgSB 0b0000_0100_0000                # the stack pointer (leave alone)\nsymbol non  0                               # stops the CPU (Breakpoint): the end of the program\n\n# The conditions of jmp\nsymbol zero  0b1000_0000_0000               # the last sum or sub gave 0\nsymbol carry 0b0100_0000_0000               # the last sum carried, or the last sub did not borrow (A >= B)\nsymbol nega  0b0010_0000_0000               # the last sub went below 0 (A < B)\nsymbol eq    0b0001_0000_0000               # A == B right now\nsymbol neq   0b0000_1000_0000               # A != B right now\n\n# ---- Macros for the common operations (explained in the README). A macro cannot use another macro, so\n#      load and store are spelled out here. v: a variable, p: a variable holding an address, L: a label ----\nmacro getA     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                                              # A = v\nmacro getB     { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6) }                                              # B = v\nmacro putA     { lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                                             # v = A (uses B)\nmacro getAat   { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                       # A = the word p points at\nmacro putAat   { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6); sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                      # the word p points at = A (uses B)\nmacro inc      { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib 1; sum; lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }  # v = v + 1 (uses A and B)\nmacro goto     { jmp; a? }                                                                    # go to L\nmacro if_eq    { jmp eq; a? }                                                                 # go to L when A == B\nmacro do       { call; a? }                                                                   # call the subroutine L\nmacro if_carry { jmp carry; a? }                                                              # go to L after a sub when A >= B\nmacro getAoff  { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib b?; sum; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                # A = the word at (p + k) (uses B)\nmacro copy     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib b?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                      # w = v (uses A and B)\nmacro get32A   { lib | (a? + 1); ld | rgAH | (rgB >> 6); lib a?; ld | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                  # A = the 32 bits at v, v + 1 (uses B)\nmacro get32B   { lia | (a? + 1); ld | rgBH | (rgA >> 6); lia a?; ld | rgB | (rgA >> 6) }                  # B = the 32 bits at v, v + 1 (uses A)\nmacro get32AB  { lib | (a? + 1); ld | rgAH | (rgB >> 6); lib a?; ld | rgA | (rgB >> 6); psh rgAH; psh rgA; lia | (b? + 1); ld | rgBH | (rgA >> 6); lia b?; ld | rgB | (rgA >> 6); pop rgA; pop rgAH }\n# get32AB v w: A = the 32 bits at v, B = the 32 bits at w\nmacro put32A   { lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6); lib | (a? + 1); sto | rgAH | (rgB >> 6) }                # the 32 bits at v, v + 1 = A (uses B)\n\nmacro keepcarry { psh rgAH; pop rgB; lia a?; sto | rgB | (rgA >> 6) }                                 # v = the upper half of A (uses A and B)\nmacro if_neq   { jmp neq; a? }                                                                # go to L when A != B\n\n\n# ---- Variables (all below 0x1000, so lia/lib can address them). NAME_H is the upper word of the 32-bit NAME ----\n# The result, four words: the largest area so far\npointer BEST0 0x0100 0\npointer BEST1 0x0101 0\npointer BEST2 0x0102 0\npointer BEST3 0x0103 0\n# R: the largest number with R * R <= 4 * BEST. A pair with W + H <= R cannot beat BEST\npointer R 0x0120 0\npointer R_H 0x0121 0\n# P, Q: the addresses of two tiles. XP, YP, XQ, YQ: copies of their coordinates\npointer P 0x0122 0x2000\npointer Q 0x0123 0\npointer XP 0x0124 0\npointer XP_H 0x0125 0\npointer YP 0x0126 0\npointer YP_H 0x0127 0\npointer XQ 0x0128 0\npointer XQ_H 0x0129 0\npointer YQ 0x012A 0\npointer YQ_H 0x012B 0\n# SP, DP: where the next addresses go in BY_S and BY_D. S_END, D_END: the addresses after their last words\npointer SP 0x012C 0x6000\npointer DP 0x012D 0x6800\npointer S_END 0x012E 0\npointer D_END 0x012F 0\n# NEW: a number on its way into the table of S and D. ADR: an address worked out for a load or a store\npointer NEW 0x0130 0\npointer NEW_H 0x0131 0\npointer ADR 0x0132 0\n# What SORT and SCAN work on: the list from LIST_BASE to LIST_END, and KEYOFF: what to add to a tile's address for its key\npointer LIST_BASE 0x0133 0\npointer LIST_END 0x0134 0\npointer KEYOFF 0x0135 0\n# For SORT: GAPP the next gap in the table, GAP the gap, IP the word being placed, JP where it may go, GP = JP - GAP\npointer GAPP 0x0136 0\npointer GAP 0x0137 0\npointer IP 0x0138 0\npointer JP 0x0139 0\npointer GP 0x013A 0\n# MOVED: the tile address being placed, KEY its key. OTHER: the tile address at GP, OTHER_KEY its key\npointer MOVED 0x013B 0\npointer KEY 0x013C 0\npointer KEY_H 0x013D 0\npointer OTHER 0x013E 0\npointer OTHER_KEY 0x013F 0\npointer OTHER_KEY_H 0x0140 0\n# For SCAN: LO and HI the two places, LAST the last place of the list, KEY_LO and KEY_HI the keys of the tiles there\npointer LO 0x0141 0\npointer HI 0x0142 0\npointer LAST 0x0143 0\npointer KEY_LO 0x0144 0\npointer KEY_LO_H 0x0145 0\npointer KEY_HI 0x0146 0\npointer KEY_HI_H 0x0147 0\n# W, H: the width and height of a rectangle. SPAN: W + H, or the most it can be\npointer W 0x0148 0\npointer W_H 0x0149 0\npointer H 0x014A 0\npointer H_H 0x014B 0\npointer SPAN 0x014C 0\npointer SPAN_H 0x014D 0\n# For MULTIPLY: AREA = W * H, MBIT the power of two being looked at, DOUBLED4 = W * MBIT\npointer AREA0 0x014E 0\npointer AREA1 0x014F 0\npointer AREA2 0x0150 0\npointer AREA3 0x0151 0\npointer MBIT 0x0152 0\npointer MBIT_H 0x0153 0\npointer DOUBLED40 0x0154 0\npointer DOUBLED41 0x0155 0\npointer DOUBLED42 0x0156 0\npointer DOUBLED43 0x0157 0\n# For NEW_R: FOUR = 4 * BEST, BP the next power of two in the table, STEP that power, TRY_R = R + STEP\npointer FOUR0 0x0158 0\npointer FOUR1 0x0159 0\npointer FOUR2 0x015A 0\npointer FOUR3 0x015B 0\npointer BP 0x015C 0\npointer STEP 0x015D 0\npointer STEP_H 0x015E 0\npointer TRY_R 0x015F 0\npointer TRY_R_H 0x0160 0\n# The carry from one word of an addition into the next\npointer CARRY 0x0161 0\n# SUBTRACT works out DIFF = X - Y, and CARRY = 1 when X >= Y\npointer X0 0x0162 0\npointer X1 0x0163 0\npointer X2 0x0164 0\npointer X3 0x0165 0\npointer Y0 0x0166 0\npointer Y1 0x0167 0\npointer Y2 0x0168 0\npointer Y3 0x0169 0\npointer DIFF0 0x016A 0\npointer DIFF1 0x016B 0\npointer DIFF2 0x016C 0\npointer DIFF3 0x016D 0\n# For the decimal digits: how often the current power of ten fitted\npointer DIGIT_VALUE 0x016E 0\n# Pointers: DIGIT the next digit to write, POW the next power of ten\npointer DIGIT 0x016F 0x0110\npointer POW 0x0170 0x1E00\n# Constants that do not fit an instruction's place for a number\npointer BY_S 0x0171 0x6000\npointer BY_D 0x0172 0x6800\npointer TO_S 0x0173 0x2000\npointer TO_D 0x0174 0x2002\npointer BITS 0x0175 0x1E40\npointer GAPS 0x0176 0x1E80\npointer END_MARK 0x0177 0xFFFF\npointer ALL_ONES 0x0178 0xFFFF\npointer BIAS 0x0179 0\npointer BIAS_H 0x017A 0x8000\n\n# ---- The CPU starts at 0xFF00 ----\norigin 0xFF00\ngoto MAIN\n\norigin 0x1000\n@ MAIN\n# ==== go through the tiles once ====\n#     while the tile at P is not the end mark:              # P starts at the first tile\n#         S[P] = x(P) + y(P); D[P] = x(P) - y(P) + 2^31\n#         memory[SP] = P; SP = SP + 1                       # BY_S\n#         memory[DP] = P; DP = DP + 1                       # BY_D\n#         P = P + 4\n#     S_END = SP; D_END = DP\n@ INIT_NEXT\n\tgetAoff P 1; getB END_MARK                                   # A = the upper word of the tile's X, B = 0xFFFF\n\tif_eq INIT_DONE                                              # the end mark: no more tiles\n\tputA XP_H                                                    # XP = x(P); upper word (A still holds it)\n\tgetAat P; putA XP                                            # lower word\n\tgetAoff P 2; putA YP                                         # YP = y(P); lower word\n\tgetAoff P 3; putA YP_H                                       # upper word\n\tget32AB XP YP; sum; put32A NEW                               # NEW = XP + YP\n\tgetA P; getB TO_S; sum; putA ADR                             # ADR = the address of the tile's S\n\tgetA NEW; putAat ADR; inc ADR                                # S[P] = NEW; lower word\n\tgetA NEW_H; putAat ADR; inc ADR                              # upper word; ADR is now the address of the tile's D\n\tget32AB XP YP; sub; put32A NEW                               # NEW = XP - YP (it may have gone below 0)\n\tget32AB NEW BIAS; sum; put32A NEW                            # NEW = NEW + 2^31, which never is\n\tgetA NEW; putAat ADR; inc ADR                                # D[P] = NEW; lower word\n\tgetA NEW_H; putAat ADR                                       # upper word\n\tgetA P; putAat SP; inc SP                                    # memory[SP] = P; SP = SP + 1\n\tgetA P; putAat DP; inc DP                                    # memory[DP] = P; DP = DP + 1\n\tgetA P; lib 4; sum; putA P                                   # P = P + 4, the next tile\n\tgoto INIT_NEXT\n@ INIT_DONE\n\tcopy SP S_END; copy DP D_END                                 # S_END, D_END = the addresses after the two lists\n\n# ==== sort both lists; try the two ends of each; scan both ====\n#     LIST = BY_S, keys S: SORT; EVALUATE(first, last)\n#     LIST = BY_D, keys D: SORT; EVALUATE(first, last)\n#     LIST = BY_D: SCAN\n#     LIST = BY_S, keys S: SCAN\n\tcopy BY_S LIST_BASE; copy S_END LIST_END; copy TO_S KEYOFF   # the list BY_S with the keys S\n\tdo SORT                                                      # sort it by S\n\tdo ENDS                                                      # EVALUATE its first and last tile\n\tcopy BY_D LIST_BASE; copy D_END LIST_END; copy TO_D KEYOFF   # the list BY_D with the keys D\n\tdo SORT                                                      # sort it by D\n\tdo ENDS                                                      # EVALUATE its first and last tile\n\tdo SCAN                                                      # the pairs far apart in D\n\tcopy BY_S LIST_BASE; copy S_END LIST_END; copy TO_S KEYOFF   # the list BY_S with the keys S\n\tdo SCAN                                                      # the pairs far apart in S\n\n# ==== write BEST as 15 decimal digits ====\n# A digit is how often a power of ten fits into what is left. Without division that is counting.\n# X is what is left of the total, Y the power of ten:\n#     X = BEST\n#     for Y in 10^14, 10^13, ..., 10, 1:                # the table ends at a number whose lowest word is 0\n#         DIGIT_VALUE = 0\n#         loop:\n#             SUBTRACT                                  # DIFF = X - Y, CARRY = 1 when X >= Y\n#             if CARRY == 0: break\n#             X = DIFF; DIGIT_VALUE = DIGIT_VALUE + 1\n#         memory[DIGIT] = DIGIT_VALUE; DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n# (The lowest word of a power of ten up to 10^15 is never 0, so a 0 there can end the table.)\n@ WRITE_BEST\n\tcopy BEST0 X0                                               # X = BEST; word 0 (the lowest)\n\tcopy BEST1 X1                                               # word 1\n\tcopy BEST2 X2                                               # word 2\n\tcopy BEST3 X3                                               # word 3 (the top)\n@ NEXT_POWER\n\tgetAat POW                                                   # A = the lowest word of the next power of ten\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0\n\tif_eq END                                                    # a lowest word of 0 ends the table\n\tputA Y0                                                      # Y = the power of ten; word 0 (the lowest)\n\tgetAoff POW 1; putA Y1                                       # word 1\n\tgetAoff POW 2; putA Y2                                       # word 2\n\tgetAoff POW 3; putA Y3                                       # word 3 (the top)\n\tgetA POW; lib 4; sum; putA POW                               # POW = POW + 4\n\tlia 0; putA DIGIT_VALUE                                      # DIGIT_VALUE = 0\n@ FITS_AGAIN\n\tdo SUBTRACT                                                  # DIFF = X - Y, CARRY = 1 when X >= Y\n\tgetA CARRY; lib 0                                            # A = CARRY, B = 0\n\tif_eq WRITE_DIGIT                                            # X < Y: the power does not fit again\n\tcopy DIFF0 X0                                                # X = DIFF; word 0 (the lowest)\n\tcopy DIFF1 X1                                                # word 1\n\tcopy DIFF2 X2                                                # word 2\n\tcopy DIFF3 X3                                                # word 3 (the top)\n\tinc DIGIT_VALUE                                              # DIGIT_VALUE = DIGIT_VALUE + 1\n\tgoto FITS_AGAIN\n@ WRITE_DIGIT\n\tgetA DIGIT_VALUE; putAat DIGIT                               # memory[DIGIT] = DIGIT_VALUE\n\tinc DIGIT                                                    # DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n\tgoto NEXT_POWER\n\n# The word 0 stops the CPU on its Breakpoint.\n@ END\n\tnon\n\n# ---- Subroutines ----\norigin 0x1800\n# ENDS: EVALUATE the first and the last tile of the list.\n@ ENDS\n\tgetAat LIST_BASE; putA P                                     # P = the first tile of the list\n\tgetA LIST_END; lib 1; sub; load rgA rgA; putA Q              # Q = the last tile of the list\n\tdo EVALUATE\n\tret\n\n# SORT: sort the list by its keys, smallest first (a Shell sort).\n# An insertion sort moves each word left past the words with a larger key, one place at a time. A Shell\n# sort does the same with a GAP: first words 1093 places apart are put in order, then 364, 121, 40, 13,\n# 4, and at last 1, when nothing has far to go any more.\n#     for GAP in 1093, 364, 121, 40, 13, 4, 1:              # from the table GAPS, which ends in 0\n#         for IP = LIST_BASE + GAP; IP < LIST_END; IP = IP + 1:\n#             MOVED = memory[IP]; KEY = key(MOVED)\n#             JP = IP\n#             loop:\n#                 GP = JP - GAP\n#                 if GP < LIST_BASE: break\n#                 OTHER = memory[GP]\n#                 if key(OTHER) <= KEY: break\n#                 memory[JP] = OTHER; JP = GP               # OTHER moves GAP places to the right\n#             memory[JP] = MOVED\n@ SORT\n\tcopy GAPS GAPP                                               # GAPP = the first gap of the table\n@ SORT_NEXT_GAP\n\tgetAat GAPP; putA GAP                                        # GAP = the next gap of the table\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0\n\tif_eq SORT_DONE                                              # the 0 that ends the table\n\tinc GAPP                                                     # GAPP = GAPP + 1\n\tgetA LIST_BASE; getB GAP; sum; putA IP                       # IP = LIST_BASE + GAP\n@ SORT_NEXT_WORD\n\tgetA IP; getB LIST_END; sub                                  # A = IP - LIST_END\n\tif_carry SORT_NEXT_GAP                                       # IP >= LIST_END: this gap is done\n\tgetAat IP; putA MOVED                                        # MOVED = memory[IP]\n\tgetA MOVED; getB KEYOFF; sum; putA ADR                       # ADR = the address of the tile's key\n\tgetAat ADR; putA KEY                                         # KEY = the key; lower word\n\tgetAoff ADR 1; putA KEY_H                                    # upper word\n\tcopy IP JP                                                   # JP = IP\n@ SORT_LEFT\n\tgetA JP; getB GAP; sub; putA GP                              # GP = JP - GAP\n\tgetB LIST_BASE; sub                                          # A = GP - LIST_BASE\n\tif_carry SORT_COMPARE                                        # GP >= LIST_BASE: there is a word GAP places to the left\n\tgoto SORT_PLACE\n@ SORT_COMPARE\n\tgetAat GP; putA OTHER                                        # OTHER = memory[GP]\n\tgetA OTHER; getB KEYOFF; sum; putA ADR                       # ADR = the address of the tile's key\n\tgetAat ADR; putA OTHER_KEY                                   # OTHER_KEY = the key; lower word\n\tgetAoff ADR 1; putA OTHER_KEY_H                              # upper word\n\tget32AB KEY OTHER_KEY; sub                                   # A = KEY - key(OTHER)\n\tif_carry SORT_PLACE                                          # key(OTHER) <= KEY: MOVED belongs at JP\n\tgetA OTHER; putAat JP                                        # memory[JP] = OTHER\n\tcopy GP JP                                                   # JP = GP\n\tgoto SORT_LEFT\n@ SORT_PLACE\n\tgetA MOVED; putAat JP                                        # memory[JP] = MOVED\n\tinc IP                                                       # IP = IP + 1\n\tgoto SORT_NEXT_WORD\n@ SORT_DONE\n\tret\n\n# SCAN: EVALUATE every pair of the sorted list whose keys are more than R - 2 apart.\n#     LAST = LIST_END - 1\n#     for LO = LIST_BASE; LO < LAST; LO = LO + 1:\n#         for HI = LAST; HI != LO; HI = HI - 1:\n#             SPAN = key(HI) - key(LO) + 2                  # W + H of this pair is SPAN at most, in this list\n#             if SPAN <= R: break                           # the pairs further in are closer still\n#             EVALUATE(tile at LO, tile at HI)\n#         if HI == LAST: return                             # nothing for this LO: nothing for any later LO\n@ SCAN\n\tgetA LIST_END; lib 1; sub; putA LAST                         # LAST = LIST_END - 1\n\tcopy LIST_BASE LO                                            # LO = the first place\n@ SCAN_LO\n\tgetA LO; getB LAST; sub                                      # A = LO - LAST\n\tif_carry SCAN_DONE                                           # LO >= LAST: no pair is left\n\tgetAat LO; putA P                                            # P = the tile at LO\n\tgetA P; getB KEYOFF; sum; putA ADR                           # ADR = the address of the tile's key\n\tgetAat ADR; putA KEY_LO                                      # KEY_LO = the key; lower word\n\tgetAoff ADR 1; putA KEY_LO_H                                 # upper word\n\tcopy LAST HI                                                 # HI = LAST\n@ SCAN_HI\n\tgetA HI; getB LO                                             # A = HI, B = LO\n\tif_eq SCAN_NEXT_LO                                           # HI has come down to LO\n\tgetAat HI; putA Q                                            # Q = the tile at HI\n\tgetA Q; getB KEYOFF; sum; putA ADR                           # ADR = the address of the tile's key\n\tgetAat ADR; putA KEY_HI                                      # KEY_HI = the key; lower word\n\tgetAoff ADR 1; putA KEY_HI_H                                 # upper word\n\tget32AB KEY_HI KEY_LO; sub; lib 2; sum; put32A SPAN          # SPAN = key(HI) - key(LO) + 2\n\tget32AB R SPAN; sub                                          # A = R - SPAN\n\tif_carry SCAN_STOP                                           # SPAN <= R: this pair and those further in cannot beat BEST\n\tdo EVALUATE\n\tgetA HI; lib 1; sub; putA HI                                 # HI = HI - 1\n\tgoto SCAN_HI\n@ SCAN_STOP\n\tgetA HI; getB LAST                                           # A = HI, B = LAST\n\tif_eq SCAN_DONE                                              # not even the last tile was far enough from LO\n@ SCAN_NEXT_LO\n\tinc LO                                                       # LO = LO + 1\n\tgoto SCAN_LO\n@ SCAN_DONE\n\tret\n\n# EVALUATE: the rectangle with the tiles at P and Q as corners; BEST and R are raised when it is larger.\n#     W = |x(P) - x(Q)| + 1; H = |y(P) - y(Q)| + 1\n#     if W + H <= R: return\n#     MULTIPLY                                              # AREA = W * H\n#     if AREA <= BEST: return\n#     BEST = AREA; NEW_R\n@ EVALUATE\n\tgetAat P; putA XP                                            # XP = x(P); lower word\n\tgetAoff P 1; putA XP_H                                       # upper word\n\tgetAoff P 2; putA YP                                         # YP = y(P); lower word\n\tgetAoff P 3; putA YP_H                                       # upper word\n\tgetAat Q; putA XQ                                            # XQ = x(Q); lower word\n\tgetAoff Q 1; putA XQ_H                                       # upper word\n\tgetAoff Q 2; putA YQ                                         # YQ = y(Q); lower word\n\tgetAoff Q 3; putA YQ_H                                       # upper word\n\tget32AB XQ XP; sub                                           # A = XQ - XP\n\tif_carry WIDTH_POSITIVE                                      # it did not go below 0\n\tneg; lib 1; sum                                              # below 0: A = -A (flip every bit, add 1)\n@ WIDTH_POSITIVE\n\tlib 1; sum; put32A W                                         # W = |XQ - XP| + 1\n\tget32AB YQ YP; sub                                           # A = YQ - YP\n\tif_carry HEIGHT_POSITIVE                                     # it did not go below 0\n\tneg; lib 1; sum                                              # below 0: A = -A\n@ HEIGHT_POSITIVE\n\tlib 1; sum; put32A H                                         # H = |YQ - YP| + 1\n\tget32AB W H; sum; put32A SPAN                                # SPAN = W + H\n\tget32AB R SPAN; sub                                          # A = R - SPAN\n\tif_carry EVALUATE_DONE                                       # W + H <= R: the area cannot beat BEST\n\tdo MULTIPLY                                                  # AREA = W * H\n\tcopy BEST0 X0; copy BEST1 X1                                 # X = BEST; words 0 and 1\n\tcopy BEST2 X2; copy BEST3 X3                                 # words 2 and 3\n\tcopy AREA0 Y0; copy AREA1 Y1                                 # Y = AREA; words 0 and 1\n\tcopy AREA2 Y2; copy AREA3 Y3                                 # words 2 and 3\n\tdo SUBTRACT                                                  # CARRY = 1 when BEST >= AREA\n\tgetA CARRY; lib 0                                            # A = CARRY, B = 0\n\tif_neq EVALUATE_DONE                                         # AREA is not larger than BEST\n\tcopy AREA0 BEST0; copy AREA1 BEST1                           # BEST = AREA; words 0 and 1\n\tcopy AREA2 BEST2; copy AREA3 BEST3                           # words 2 and 3\n\tdo NEW_R                                                     # R = the largest number with R * R <= 4 * BEST\n@ EVALUATE_DONE\n\tret\n\n# MULTIPLY: AREA = W * H. As on paper, in binary: H is a sum of powers of two, and W * H is the sum of\n# W * (those powers).\n#     AREA = 0; DOUBLED4 = W; MBIT = 1\n#     while MBIT <= H:\n#         if (H and MBIT) != 0: AREA = AREA + DOUBLED4\n#         DOUBLED4 = DOUBLED4 + DOUBLED4                    # DOUBLED4 is W * MBIT\n#         MBIT = MBIT * 2\n@ MULTIPLY\n\tlia 0; putA AREA0; putA AREA1; putA AREA2; putA AREA3        # AREA = 0, all four words\n\tputA DOUBLED42; putA DOUBLED43; putA MBIT_H                  # the upper words of DOUBLED4 and MBIT = 0 (A still holds 0)\n\tcopy W DOUBLED40; copy W_H DOUBLED41                         # DOUBLED4 = W\n\tlia 1; putA MBIT                                             # MBIT = 1\n@ MULTIPLY_ROUND\n\tget32AB H MBIT; sub                                          # A = H - MBIT\n\tif_carry MULTIPLY_BIT                                        # H >= MBIT: there may be more to add\n\tret\n@ MULTIPLY_BIT\n\tget32AB H MBIT; and                                          # A = H and MBIT\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0\n\tif_eq MULTIPLY_DOUBLE                                        # H does not have this power of two\n\tlia 0; putA CARRY                                            # CARRY = 0\n\tgetA AREA0; getB DOUBLED40; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA AREA0; keepcarry CARRY # AREA = AREA + DOUBLED4; word 0: AREA0 = AREA0 + DOUBLED40 + carry\n\tgetA AREA1; getB DOUBLED41; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA AREA1; keepcarry CARRY # word 1\n\tgetA AREA2; getB DOUBLED42; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA AREA2; keepcarry CARRY # word 2\n\tgetA AREA3; getB DOUBLED43; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA AREA3 # word 3 (the top)\n@ MULTIPLY_DOUBLE\n\tlia 0; putA CARRY                                            # CARRY = 0\n\tgetA DOUBLED40; getB DOUBLED40; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DOUBLED40; keepcarry CARRY # DOUBLED4 = DOUBLED4 + DOUBLED4; word 0: DOUBLED40 = DOUBLED40 + DOUBLED40 + carry\n\tgetA DOUBLED41; getB DOUBLED41; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DOUBLED41; keepcarry CARRY # word 1\n\tgetA DOUBLED42; getB DOUBLED42; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DOUBLED42; keepcarry CARRY # word 2\n\tgetA DOUBLED43; getB DOUBLED43; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DOUBLED43 # word 3 (the top)\n\tget32A MBIT; shf | 1; put32A MBIT                            # MBIT = MBIT * 2\n\tgoto MULTIPLY_ROUND\n\n# NEW_R: R = the largest number with R * R <= 4 * BEST, built bit by bit from the top bit down.\n#     FOUR = BEST + BEST; FOUR = FOUR + FOUR\n#     R = 0\n#     for STEP in 2^20, 2^19, ..., 2, 1:                    # from the table BITS, which ends in 0\n#         TRY_R = R + STEP\n#         W = H = TRY_R; MULTIPLY                           # AREA = TRY_R * TRY_R\n#         if AREA <= FOUR: R = TRY_R\n# (W, H and AREA are free for this: EVALUATE is done with them.)\n@ NEW_R\n\tlia 0; putA CARRY                                            # CARRY = 0\n\tgetA BEST0; getB BEST0; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA FOUR0; keepcarry CARRY # FOUR = BEST + BEST; word 0: FOUR0 = BEST0 + BEST0 + carry\n\tgetA BEST1; getB BEST1; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA FOUR1; keepcarry CARRY # word 1\n\tgetA BEST2; getB BEST2; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA FOUR2; keepcarry CARRY # word 2\n\tgetA BEST3; getB BEST3; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA FOUR3     # word 3 (the top)\n\tlia 0; putA CARRY                                            # CARRY = 0\n\tgetA FOUR0; getB FOUR0; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA FOUR0; keepcarry CARRY # FOUR = FOUR + FOUR; word 0: FOUR0 = FOUR0 + FOUR0 + carry\n\tgetA FOUR1; getB FOUR1; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA FOUR1; keepcarry CARRY # word 1\n\tgetA FOUR2; getB FOUR2; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA FOUR2; keepcarry CARRY # word 2\n\tgetA FOUR3; getB FOUR3; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA FOUR3     # word 3 (the top)\n\tlia 0; putA R; putA R_H                                      # R = 0\n\tcopy BITS BP                                                 # BP = the first power of two of the table\n@ NEW_R_STEP\n\tgetAat BP; putA STEP                                         # STEP = the next power of two; lower word\n\tgetAoff BP 1; putA STEP_H                                    # upper word\n\tget32A STEP; lib 0                                           # A = STEP, B = 0\n\tif_eq NEW_R_DONE                                             # the 0 that ends the table\n\tgetA BP; lib 2; sum; putA BP                                 # BP = BP + 2\n\tget32AB R STEP; sum; put32A TRY_R                            # TRY_R = R + STEP\n\tcopy TRY_R W; copy TRY_R_H W_H                               # W = TRY_R\n\tcopy TRY_R H; copy TRY_R_H H_H                               # H = TRY_R\n\tdo MULTIPLY                                                  # AREA = TRY_R * TRY_R\n\tcopy FOUR0 X0; copy FOUR1 X1                                 # X = FOUR; words 0 and 1\n\tcopy FOUR2 X2; copy FOUR3 X3                                 # words 2 and 3\n\tcopy AREA0 Y0; copy AREA1 Y1                                 # Y = AREA; words 0 and 1\n\tcopy AREA2 Y2; copy AREA3 Y3                                 # words 2 and 3\n\tdo SUBTRACT                                                  # CARRY = 1 when FOUR >= AREA\n\tgetA CARRY; lib 0                                            # A = CARRY, B = 0\n\tif_eq NEW_R_STEP                                             # TRY_R * TRY_R is above 4 * BEST: the bit does not stay\n\tcopy TRY_R R; copy TRY_R_H R_H                               # R = TRY_R\n\tgoto NEW_R_STEP\n@ NEW_R_DONE\n\tret\n\n# SUBTRACT: DIFF = X - Y, and CARRY = 1 when X >= Y (0 when X < Y; DIFF is then of no use).\n# Done as X + (Y with every bit flipped) + 1, word by word from the lowest:\n#     CARRY = 1                                         # the \"+ 1\"\n#     for k in 0, 1, 2, 3:\n#         A = (0xFFFF - Yk) + Xk + CARRY                # at most 0x1FFFF\n#         DIFFk = the lower half of A; CARRY = the upper half of A\n# X and Y are left as they were.\n@ SUBTRACT\n\tlia 1; putA CARRY                                            # CARRY = 1\n\tgetA ALL_ONES; getB Y0; sub; getB X0; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DIFF0; keepcarry CARRY # word 0: DIFF0 = (0xFFFF - Y0) + X0 + carry\n\tgetA ALL_ONES; getB Y1; sub; getB X1; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DIFF1; keepcarry CARRY # word 1\n\tgetA ALL_ONES; getB Y2; sub; getB X2; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DIFF2; keepcarry CARRY # word 2\n\tgetA ALL_ONES; getB Y3; sub; getB X3; sum; getB CARRY; sum; putA DIFF3; keepcarry CARRY # word 3: its carry is 1 when X >= Y\n\tret\n\n# ---- Powers of ten for the decimal digits: 10^14 down to 1, four words each, lowest first; then 0 ----\norigin 0x1E00\n0x4000; 0x107A; 0x5AF3; 0x0000    # 100000000000000\n0xA000; 0x4E72; 0x0918; 0x0000    # 10000000000000\n0x1000; 0xD4A5; 0x00E8; 0x0000    # 1000000000000\n0xE800; 0x4876; 0x0017; 0x0000    # 100000000000\n0xE400; 0x540B; 0x0002; 0x0000    # 10000000000\n0xCA00; 0x3B9A; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 1000000000\n0xE100; 0x05F5; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 100000000\n0x9680; 0x0098; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 10000000\n0x4240; 0x000F; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 1000000\n0x86A0; 0x0001; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 100000\n0x2710; 0x0000; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 10000\n0x03E8; 0x0000; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 1000\n0x0064; 0x0000; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 100\n0x000A; 0x0000; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 10\n0x0001; 0x0000; 0x0000; 0x0000    # 1\n0\n\n\n# ---- Powers of two for NEW_R: 2^20 down to 1, two words each, lower first; then 0, 0 ----\norigin 0x1E40\n0x0000; 0x0010    # 2^20\n0x0000; 0x0008    # 2^19\n0x0000; 0x0004    # 2^18\n0x0000; 0x0002    # 2^17\n0x0000; 0x0001    # 2^16\n0x8000; 0x0000    # 2^15\n0x4000; 0x0000    # 2^14\n0x2000; 0x0000    # 2^13\n0x1000; 0x0000    # 2^12\n0x0800; 0x0000    # 2^11\n0x0400; 0x0000    # 2^10\n0x0200; 0x0000    # 2^9\n0x0100; 0x0000    # 2^8\n0x0080; 0x0000    # 2^7\n0x0040; 0x0000    # 2^6\n0x0020; 0x0000    # 2^5\n0x0010; 0x0000    # 2^4\n0x0008; 0x0000    # 2^3\n0x0004; 0x0000    # 2^2\n0x0002; 0x0000    # 2^1\n0x0001; 0x0000    # 2^0\n0; 0\n\n# ---- The gaps of the sort, then 0 ----\norigin 0x1E80\n1093; 364; 121; 40; 13; 4; 1; 0\n\n# ---- The red tiles: 496, then the end mark ----\n# Four words per tile: X, then Y, each lower word first.\norigin 0x2000\n0x7D4F; 0x0001; 0xC4B7; 0x0000    # 97615,50359\n0x7D4F; 0x0001; 0xC976; 0x0000    # 97615,51574\n0x7E58; 0x0001; 0xC976; 0x0000    # 97880,51574\n0x7E58; 0x0001; 0xCE33; 0x0000    # 97880,52787\n0x7E28; 0x0001; 0xCE33; 0x0000    # 97832,52787\n0x7E28; 0x0001; 0xD31D; 0x0000    # 97832,54045\n0x8004; 0x0001; 0xD31D; 0x0000    # 98308,54045\n0x8004; 0x0001; 0xD793; 0x0000    # 98308,55187\n0x7CAF; 0x0001; 0xD793; 0x0000    # 97455,55187\n0x7CAF; 0x0001; 0xDC6C; 0x0000    # 97455,56428\n0x7D48; 0x0001; 0xDC6C; 0x0000    # 97608,56428\n0x7D48; 0x0001; 0xE0E9; 0x0000    # 97608,57577\n0x7B3E; 0x0001; 0xE0E9; 0x0000    # 97086,57577\n0x7B3E; 0x0001; 0xE5B0; 0x0000    # 97086,58800\n0x7B1E; 0x0001; 0xE5B0; 0x0000    # 97054,58800\n0x7B1E; 0x0001; 0xEA98; 0x0000    # 97054,60056\n0x7B68; 0x0001; 0xEA98; 0x0000    # 97128,60056\n0x7B68; 0x0001; 0xEF63; 0x0000    # 97128,61283\n0x7AF3; 0x0001; 0xEF63; 0x0000    # 97011,61283\n0x7AF3; 0x0001; 0xF398; 0x0000    # 97011,62360\n0x7827; 0x0001; 0xF398; 0x0000    # 96295,62360\n0x7827; 0x0001; 0xF7F2; 0x0000    # 96295,63474\n0x7624; 0x0001; 0xF7F2; 0x0000    # 95780,63474\n0x7624; 0x0001; 0xFCCE; 0x0000    # 95780,64718\n0x75D6; 0x0001; 0xFCCE; 0x0000    # 95702,64718\n0x75D6; 0x0001; 0x00DE; 0x0001    # 95702,65758\n0x730E; 0x0001; 0x00DE; 0x0001    # 94990,65758